## Registration crashes when redirecting to account confirmation

### Status
Fixed.

### GitHub Issue
None yet.

### Summary
`RegisterModel.OnPostAsync` redirects to a Razor Page (`RegisterConfirmation`) that was never
scaffolded in this project, so every successful registration now throws once
`RequireConfirmedAccount` is `true` (see `Spec/Features/FEATURES-email-verification.ospec`, #94).
Fixing the redirect is also the right moment to consolidate two pages that now say effectively
the same thing to a user in different flows.

### Reported Behavior
Registering a new account with correct, valid input throws
`InvalidOperationException: No page named 'RegisterConfirmation' matches the supplied values`
instead of completing.

### Expected Behavior
After a successful registration, the user lands on a real confirmation page telling them to
check their email, with no server error. A login or external-login attempt against an
unconfirmed account lands on that same page.

### Root Cause
`Register.cshtml.cs`'s stock scaffolded branch —
`if (_userManager.Options.SignIn.RequireConfirmedAccount) return RedirectToPage("RegisterConfirmation", ...)`
— was dead code while `RequireConfirmedAccount` was `false`. #94 flipped that flag to `true`
without noticing `RegisterConfirmation.cshtml`/`.cshtml.cs` had never been scaffolded/kept in
this repo, so the branch now executes and fails to resolve.

Separately, #94 already built `ConfirmEmailRequired.cshtml` — a hand-written page serving the
same "you must confirm before you can log in" purpose for `Login.cshtml.cs` and
`ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`'s `IsNotAllowed` branches — so there are now two different pages that
would tell a user the same thing in different flows.

### Proposed Fix
Consolidate to one page, using the stock scaffolded `RegisterConfirmation` page rather than the
hand-built one (chosen so the app leans on Identity's own scaffolding instead of a
parallel custom page):
1. Scaffold `RegisterConfirmation.cshtml`/`.cshtml.cs` for real.
2. The stock template defaults `DisplayConfirmAccountLink = true`, which prints the raw
   confirmation link directly on the page instead of requiring the user to open their email.
   This must be set `false` outright — not merely gated to `Development` — since confirming by
   actually clicking the emailed link is a hard requirement here, not a testing convenience to
   preserve.
3. Add the same "Resend email confirmation" link `ConfirmEmailRequired.cshtml` currently has.
4. Delete `ConfirmEmailRequired.cshtml`/`.cshtml.cs`.
5. Update `Login.cshtml.cs`'s `IsNotAllowed` branch to redirect to `RegisterConfirmation`
   (passing the account's known email) instead of `ConfirmEmailRequired`.
6. Update `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`'s `OnGetCallbackAsync` `IsNotAllowed` branch and
   `OnPostConfirmationAsync`'s unverified-email branch the same way.

### Acceptance Criteria
- Registering a new account with `RequireConfirmedAccount = true` succeeds and lands on
  `RegisterConfirmation` with no server error.
- `RegisterConfirmation` never displays a raw confirmation link or otherwise lets an account be
  confirmed without the user actually opening the email.
- `RegisterConfirmation` offers a "resend confirmation" link.
- Login with an unconfirmed account (correct password) redirects to `RegisterConfirmation`.
- External-login (Google) sign-in against an account whose email isn't confirmed redirects to
  `RegisterConfirmation`.
- `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`'s email-mismatch-on-confirmation path redirects to
  `RegisterConfirmation`.
- `ConfirmEmailRequired.cshtml`/`.cshtml.cs` no longer exists.
- A new end-to-end test drives `RegisterModel.OnPostAsync` with `RequireConfirmedAccount = true`
  and asserts a successful redirect to `RegisterConfirmation` — this is the test that would have
  caught the original crash.

### Out of Scope
- The confirmation-email-sending logic itself (token generation, callback URL shape) — covered by
  `Spec/Refactors/REFACTOR-signin-manager-and-confirmation-email.ospec`'s `EmailConfirmationLinkSender`
  extraction.
- Lockout-related behavior changes — deferred to a future issue.
